  "Question": "If \\(\\log_7(\\log_3(\\log_2x))=0,\\) then \\(x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\\) equals",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(\\frac13\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\frac1{2\\sqrt3}\\)",
    "C": "\\(\\frac1{3\\sqrt3}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac1{\\sqrt{42}}\\)",
    "E": "none of these"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "E",
  "Solution": "The definition of a logarithm lets us undo the nesting from the outside inward. First, the logarithms require \\(x>0\\), \\(\\log_2x>0\\), and \\(\\log_3(\\log_2x)>0\\). Together these conditions mean \\(x>2\\).\n\nUsing \\(\\log_a b=c\\) to mean \\(b=a^c\\), we obtain\n\\[\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\log_3(\\log_2x)&=7^0=1,\\\\\n\\log_2x&=3^1=3,\\\\\nx&=2^3=8.\n\\end{aligned}\n\\]\nThis value satisfies \\(x>2\\), so all the original logarithms are defined. Therefore\n\\[\nx^{-1/2}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt8}\n=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt2}\n=\\frac{\\sqrt2}{4}.\n\\]\nThe positive denominators in choices A through D have squares \\(9,12,27,42\\), respectively, whereas our denominator has square \\(8\\). Thus the answer is \\(\\frac{\\sqrt2}{4}\\), choice E.",
